WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:09.283
A DuckLake supports not null, but no primary keys, no unique constraints, and no foreign keys.

00:00:09.563 --> 00:00:12.094
Nothing stops a duplicate block from getting in.

00:00:13.044 --> 00:00:16.999
So we check those rules with dbt tests, every time we build.

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:20.115
In models, create schema dot yml.

00:00:20.395 --> 00:00:32.308
Descriptions for the models, unique and not null on the keys, accepted values on the success flag, and a relationship: every transaction's block number must exist in stg blocks.

00:00:33.258 --> 00:00:39.340
Run dbt build: it builds every model in order, and runs each model's tests right after it.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.043
Five models and nine tests: fourteen passes in all.

00:00:47.577 --> 00:00:48.515
Now the docs.

00:00:48.795 --> 00:00:56.128
Project menu, Generate Docs. dbt reads the project and the lake, and writes a small website into target.

00:00:58.687 --> 00:01:00.254
Then Project, Serve Docs.

00:01:00.534 --> 00:01:03.706
Studio starts a local web server, on port eighty eighty.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:10.760
Open it in the browser, on the daily gas model: its description, and its columns.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:19.776
And the lineage graph for this model: daily gas is built from stg transactions, the staging view on the raw transactions table.

00:01:20.055 --> 00:01:25.994
The full graph of the project, from both raw tables to all three marts, opens with the button at the top.

00:01:26.944 --> 00:01:29.243
Commit the tests and descriptions, and push.

00:01:41.602 --> 00:01:49.407
To recap: the lake does not enforce unique keys, so dbt tests do, and dbt build runs them with every model.

00:01:49.687 --> 00:01:53.575
And the docs show each model, its columns and tests, and its lineage.

00:01:53.855 --> 00:01:55.300
That ends Module 6.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.745
One lesson left: where to go from here.
